    {
      "title": "Positioning",
      "slug": "positioning",
      "displayOrder": 3,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 797,
        "lessonOrder": 3
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\n\r\n\r\nBy default, our knitting machines position stitch patterns in the middle of the needlebed.<br><ul>\r\n<li>\r\nElectronic machines allow us to easily shift the pattern to any needle on the bed and allow us to choose the number of repeats to knit.</li>\r\n<li>Punchcard machines can be manipulated with special cams and accessories to precisely position a stitch pattern.</li>\r\n<li>DesignaKnit will also position stitch patterns</li>\r\n</ul><br>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"yellow_highlight\"><b>Check your manual for specific instructions for your machine.</b></div>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/119/1 repeat.jpg\"><br>\r\n\r\n\t<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/119/position.jpg\">\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 9647,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Position a Stitch Pattern",
          "slug": "position-a-stitch-pattern",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 6297,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Floats",
      "slug": "floats",
      "displayOrder": 4,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 798,
        "lessonOrder": 4
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<style>\r\n    .container-border1 {\r\n        border: 2px solid #ccc;\r\n        border-radius: 15px 50px;\r\n        padding-top: 20px;\r\n        padding-right: 20px;\r\n        padding-bottom: 20px;\r\n        padding-left: 20px;\r\n\r\n        max-width: 500px;\r\n        margin: auto;\r\n        background-color: #DCDCDC;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n</style>\r\n\r\n\r\n    \r\n        To avoid unwanted long floats when selecting stitch patterns, \"read\" the pattern before knitting.<br><div class=\"container-border1\">\r\n            Which of these 2 stitch patterns will have the shortest floats?<br><em> <b>A</b> has only 3 stitch floats<br><b> B</b> has a row with 11 stitch floats </em>\r\n        </div>\r\n        \r\n            \r\n                <h3><b>A</b></h3>\r\n                <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/119/punchcard2.jpg\"><br>\r\n            \r\n\r\n            \r\n                <h3><b>B</b></h3><img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/119/Copy of punchcard1_1.jpg\">\r\n            \r\n        \r\n\r\n    \r\n    \r\n    \r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 9659,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Recognizing Floats",
          "slug": "recognizing-floats",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 6302,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<style>\r\n.play1 {\r\nposition: relative;\r\nwidth: 100%;\r\nmax-width: 400px;\r\n}\r\n.play1 img {\r\nwidth: 100%;\r\nheight: auto;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.play1 .btn {\r\nposition: absolute;\r\ntop: 50%;\r\nleft: 50%;\r\ntransform: translate(-50%, -50%);\r\n-ms-transform: translate(-50%, -50%);\r\nbackground-color: #555;\r\ncolor: white;\r\nfont-size: 16px;\r\npadding: 12px 24px;\r\nborder: none;\r\ncursor: pointer;\r\nborder-radius: 5px;\r\ntext-align: center;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.play1 .btn:hover {\r\nbackground-color: black;\r\n}\r\n    .container-border1 {\r\n        border: 2px solid #ccc;\r\n        border-radius: 15px 50px;\r\n        padding-top: 20px;\r\n        padding-right: 20px;\r\n        padding-bottom: 20px;\r\n        padding-left: 20px;\r\n\r\n        max-width: 500px;\r\n        margin: auto;\r\n        background-color: #DCDCDC;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n</style>\r\n\r\n\r\n    \r\n\r\n        Many knitters shy away from Jacquard (fairisle) knitting because of the floats that are formed. Unlike hand knitters, we can't twist the working strands and prevent the floats as we knit. But there are many things machine knitters can do to \"tame\" floats.<br>Floats refers to strands of yarn carried across the purl side of the knitting.<br>\r\n        When Knitting fairisle, the opposite color of yarn is carried behind the stitches that are knit.\r\n\r\n\r\n        <br>\r\n        <div class=\"container-border1\">\r\n            <ol>\r\n                <li>Cast on and knit in a stitch pattern with long floats\r\n                    <ul>\r\n                        <li>Latch up the floats as you knit</li>\r\n                        <li>Weave the floats on the next row</li>\r\n                        <li>Continue knitting for a few more inches and bind off</li>\r\n                        <li>Try using a sewing thread to secure the floats</li>\r\n                    </ul>\r\n            </ol>\r\n\r\n\r\n        </div><br>\r\n       \r\n    \r\n    \r\n        <img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/images/glossary/floats.JPG\">\r\n    \r\n\r\n<h3> Watch the Video</h3>\r\n<div class=\"play1\">\r\n<div class=\"videopopup\" data-videotitle=\"Managing Floats\" data-vimeoid=\"475492261\" style=\"width:100%\">\r\n    <img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"https://i.vimeocdn.com/video/988244244-1801b21cd167065fd204fa0491fdf02bcf768a71dd510e2cecfcd39a0d1a1cc6-d_1280x720?r=pad\">\r\n    <div class=\"imagecenter text-center\"><button class=\"btn btn-sm\"><i class=\"fa fa-youtube-play fa-2x learn\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></button></div>\r\n</div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div style=\"margin: 50px;\"><h3>Suggestions for dealing with floats</h3></div>\r\n\r\n\r\n<ul class=\"fa-ul\">\r\n    <li><span class=\"fa-li\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"></i></span>Embrace the floats with slip</li>\r\n    <li><span class=\"fa-li\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"></i></span>Add a lining</li>\r\n    <li><span class=\"fa-li\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"></i></span>Doubled scarf or blanket</li>\r\n    <li><span class=\"fa-li\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"></i></span>Latch the strands as you knit</li></ul>\r\n <ul class=\"fa-ul\">   <li><span class=\"fa-li\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"></i></span>Secure by sewing</li>\r\n    <li><span class=\"fa-li\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"></i></span>Weave in as you knit</li>\r\n    <li><span class=\"fa-li\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"></i></span>Modify the stitch pattern</li>\r\n    <li><span class=\"fa-li\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"></i></span>Consider intarsia</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 9658,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Dealing with Floats",
          "slug": "dealing-with-floats",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
